VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — When diluted EPS equals basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, in which situation will a company report diluted earnings per share equal to basic earnings per share?', '["When the company has convertible bonds outstanding", "When the company declares a stock dividend during the year", "When the company has no potentially dilutive securities outstanding", "When the company reports comprehensive income in addition to net income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, diluted EPS equals basic EPS when there are no potentially dilutive securities such as options, warrants, or convertible instruments outstanding, because the diluted computation is identical to the basic computation. Choice A is incorrect because convertible bonds are potentially dilutive securities that require the if-converted method, typically causing diluted EPS to differ from basic EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — Contingently issuable RSUs with unmet performance condition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Foxworth Technologies granted 40,000 restricted stock units to executives on January 1, Year 1, contingent on achieving cumulative revenue of $20 million by December 31, Year 3. At December 31, Year 1, cumulative revenue was $7.8 million, and management believes the target will be met. Foxworth has 300,000 common shares and net income of $900,000. Analyze how these contingently issuable shares should be treated in the Year 1 diluted EPS calculation.', '["Excluded from both basic and diluted EPS because the revenue condition would not be satisfied if December 31, Year 1 were the end of the contingency period", "Included in diluted EPS for 40,000 shares because management believes the target will ultimately be achieved", "Included in basic EPS on a pro rata basis of 15,600 shares (40,000 multiplied by $7.8 million divided by $20 million)", "Included in diluted EPS for 40,000 shares because any contingency with a probability above 50% must be included"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48 and 45-52, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS only if all conditions would be satisfied assuming the end of the reporting period were the end of the contingency period. Since $7.8 million is below the $20 million target, the shares are excluded. Choice B is incorrect because management''s expectation of future achievement does not override the period-end test prescribed by ASC 260 for contingently issuable shares.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — No EPS required for comprehensive income
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, is an entity required to present per-share amounts for total comprehensive income?', '["Yes, per-share amounts must be presented for all components of comprehensive income", "Yes, but only when other comprehensive income exceeds 10% of net income", "Yes, total comprehensive income EPS is required alongside net income EPS", "No, per-share presentation is not required for comprehensive income or its individual components"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, per-share amounts are required only for income from continuing operations and net income. There is no requirement to present EPS for comprehensive income or individual components of other comprehensive income. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly limits the per-share presentation requirement to income statement line items, not comprehensive income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Shares issued mid-year: weighted-average calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakeview Manufacturing had 200,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On April 1, Lakeview issued 60,000 additional shares for cash. Net income for the year was $612,500 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$3.06", "$2.50", "$2.36", "$2.72"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares are weighted for the fraction of the period outstanding. Weighted-average = (200,000 x 3/12) + (260,000 x 9/12) = 50,000 + 195,000 = 245,000 shares. Basic EPS = $612,500 / 245,000 = $2.50. Choice A ($3.06) is incorrect because it divides by the beginning share count of 200,000 rather than the weighted-average that reflects the April 1 issuance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Out-of-the-money options excluded from diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Riverton Corp. has 180,000 weighted-average common shares, net income of $540,000, and 10,000 stock options with an exercise price of $75. The average market price during the year was $60. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.00, identical to basic EPS, because the options are antidilutive and excluded", "$2.84 after adding 10,000 incremental shares to the denominator", "$2.70 after adding 20,000 incremental shares to the denominator", "$3.16 because the options reduce the diluted denominator"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, stock options are excluded from diluted EPS when the exercise price exceeds the average market price (out-of-the-money) because their inclusion would be antidilutive. Diluted EPS equals basic EPS of $540,000 / 180,000 = $3.00. Choice B is incorrect because including out-of-the-money options via the treasury stock method would produce negative incremental shares, which would increase EPS rather than decrease it.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Sequential dilution testing with antidilutive cutoff
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Claremont Industries reports net income of $1,500,000 with 300,000 common shares. Three potentially dilutive securities exist: Security P consists of 20,000 stock options (exercise price $25, average market price $50) producing 10,000 incremental shares with per-share impact of $0. Security Q is $400,000 face value of 5% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%) converting into 16,000 shares with per-share impact of $0.94. Security R is 4,000 shares of $100 par, 6% convertible preferred stock converting into 4,000 common shares with per-share impact of $6.00. Evaluate the proper inclusion sequence and diluted EPS.', '["$4.66, including all three securities because each has a positive per-share impact", "$4.84, including only P because both Q and R are antidilutive when tested sequentially", "$4.65, including P and Q but excluding R, because R''s per-share impact of $6.00 exceeds the cumulative diluted EPS after including P and Q, making R antidilutive at that step", "$4.65, including P and R but excluding Q because convertible preferred securities have priority over convertible debt"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, securities are ranked by per-share impact (lowest first): P ($0), Q ($0.94), R ($6.00). After P: $1,500,000 / 310,000 = $4.84. After Q: $1,515,000 / 326,000 = $4.65. Testing R: per-share impact $6.00 exceeds $4.65, so inclusion would increase EPS — R is antidilutive and excluded. Choice A is incorrect because including R would increase diluted EPS from $4.65 to $4.66, violating the rule that diluted EPS must not exceed basic EPS at any step.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Noncumulative preferred dividends not declared: numerator effect
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bellwood Corp. has 100,000 shares of $50 par, 5% noncumulative preferred stock and 500,000 common shares outstanding. Net income is $1,200,000. The board of directors did not declare preferred dividends in the current year. Analyze the effect on the basic EPS numerator compared to a year when the full $250,000 in dividends is declared.', '["The numerator is reduced by $250,000 in both years because the preferred dividend accrues regardless of declaration", "The numerator is $1,200,000 in both years because preferred dividends never affect the EPS calculation", "The numerator is reduced by $250,000 in both years because ASC 260 always requires deducting the stated rate for outstanding preferred stock", "The numerator is $1,200,000 when undeclared because noncumulative dividends are deducted only when declared, but would be $950,000 in a year when the $250,000 is declared"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, noncumulative preferred dividends reduce the EPS numerator only when declared by the board. When undeclared, the full net income of $1,200,000 is available to common shareholders. In contrast, cumulative preferred dividends must be deducted regardless of declaration. Choice A is incorrect because the accrual-regardless-of-declaration rule applies only to cumulative preferred stock, not noncumulative preferred stock.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Complex capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what characterizes a complex capital structure for EPS reporting purposes?', '["Total equity exceeds $50 million", "The entity has outstanding potentially dilutive securities such as convertible bonds, options, or warrants", "The entity has more than one class of common stock authorized in its charter", "The entity is listed on a national securities exchange"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS, such as convertible debt, convertible preferred stock, stock options, or warrants. Choice C is incorrect because having multiple classes of common stock authorized does not automatically create a complex capital structure unless one class has conversion features or other dilutive potential.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Convertible bonds: if-converted method full year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cedarwood Corp. has net income of $960,000 and 300,000 weighted-average common shares. It has $400,000 face value of 6% convertible bonds outstanding for the entire year. Each $1,000 bond converts into 25 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. What is diluted EPS using the if-converted method?', '["$3.15", "$3.20", "$3.06", "$3.26"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back after-tax interest to the numerator and adds converted shares to the denominator. After-tax interest = $400,000 x 6% x (1 - 0.25) = $18,000. Conversion shares = 400 bonds x 25 = 10,000. Diluted EPS = ($960,000 + $18,000) / (300,000 + 10,000) = $978,000 / 310,000 = $3.15. Choice B ($3.20) is incorrect because it equals basic EPS ($960,000 / 300,000), failing to apply the if-converted adjustments for the bonds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — New share issuance combined with preferred dividend deduction
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stratford Holdings had 250,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On July 1, Stratford issued 50,000 additional shares. Net income was $720,000. Stratford has cumulative preferred stock with an annual dividend of $60,000. What is basic EPS?', '["$2.64", "$2.20", "$2.40", "$2.88"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10 and 45-11, the weighted-average shares are (250,000 x 6/12) + (300,000 x 6/12) = 125,000 + 150,000 = 275,000. The numerator deducts cumulative preferred dividends: $720,000 - $60,000 = $660,000. Basic EPS = $660,000 / 275,000 = $2.40. Choice A ($2.64) is incorrect because it uses the beginning share count of 250,000 without weighting for the mid-year issuance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Meaning of income available to common shareholders
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'In the context of computing basic EPS under ASC 260, what does the term "income available to common shareholders" represent?', '["Net income minus preferred dividends applicable to the period", "Net income plus other comprehensive income", "Gross profit minus operating expenses and preferred dividends", "Net income minus common dividends declared during the period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, income available to common shareholders equals net income minus preferred dividends (declared for noncumulative, accumulated for cumulative). Common dividends are not deducted because they represent a distribution of earnings already captured in net income. Choice D is incorrect because common dividends reduce retained earnings but are not subtracted from the EPS numerator.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Options at exercise price equal to market price produce zero dilution
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under the treasury stock method in ASC 260, what is the dilutive effect when 10,000 stock options have an exercise price of $50 and the average market price during the period is also $50?', '["The options add 10,000 shares to the denominator because all options are assumed exercised", "The options add 5,000 incremental shares because half the proceeds are used for repurchase", "The options reduce the share count because the exercise proceeds exceed the market value of shares issued", "The options produce zero incremental shares and have no dilutive effect on EPS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes exercise proceeds ($500,000) repurchase shares at the average market price ($50), buying back all 10,000 shares issued. The net incremental shares are zero, producing no dilution. Choice A is incorrect because the method nets newly issued shares against shares deemed repurchased, and when exercise price equals market price, these amounts exactly offset.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Treasury stock repurchase reduces weighted-average shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Colton Enterprises had 400,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On April 1, Colton repurchased 48,000 shares as treasury stock. No other share transactions occurred during the year. Net income was $546,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$1.37", "$1.50", "$1.55", "$1.43"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury stock reduces shares outstanding from the repurchase date. Weighted-average = (400,000 x 3/12) + (352,000 x 9/12) = 100,000 + 264,000 = 364,000. Basic EPS = $546,000 / 364,000 = $1.50. Choice A ($1.37) is incorrect because it divides by the full 400,000 beginning share count without reducing for the April 1 repurchase.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Shares issued at two dates during the year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgemont Corp. had 300,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On July 1, Ridgemont issued 60,000 new shares. Net income was $660,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$2.20", "$1.83", "$1.94", "$2.00"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares are weighted for the period outstanding. Weighted-average = (300,000 x 6/12) + (360,000 x 6/12) = 150,000 + 180,000 = 330,000. Basic EPS = $660,000 / 330,000 = $2.00. Choice A ($2.20) is incorrect because it uses only the beginning share count of 300,000, ignoring the weighted-average requirement for the mid-year issuance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Rising average market price increases treasury stock method dilution
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sentinel Corp. has 25,000 stock options outstanding with an exercise price of $40. In Year 1, the average market price was $50, producing 5,000 incremental shares under the treasury stock method. In Year 2, the average market price rose to $80. Analyze the effect of the higher average market price on the incremental shares for Year 2.', '["Incremental shares decrease because a higher market price means each repurchased share is more expensive, reducing the dilutive effect", "Incremental shares remain unchanged at 5,000 because the exercise price and number of options have not changed", "Incremental shares increase from 5,000 to 12,500 because the higher market price reduces the number of shares assumed repurchased with the fixed exercise proceeds, increasing dilution", "The options become antidilutive at the higher market price because the gap between exercise price and market price is too large"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method uses fixed exercise proceeds ($1,000,000) to repurchase shares at the average market price. At $50, repurchase = 20,000 shares, incremental = 5,000. At $80, repurchase = 12,500 shares, incremental = 12,500. The higher market price means fewer shares can be repurchased, increasing dilution. Choice A is incorrect because while each repurchased share costs more, this reduces the number of shares bought back, resulting in more incremental dilutive shares.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Definition of potential common stock
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following best describes a "potential common share"?', '["A security or contract that may entitle its holder to obtain common shares, such as stock options, warrants, or convertible instruments", "Any share of stock that has been authorized but not yet issued by the corporation", "A share of treasury stock that the company may reissue in the future", "A share of common stock held by an officer or director subject to a lock-up agreement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, a potential common share is a security or other contract that may entitle the holder to obtain common shares during the reporting period or after, including options, warrants, convertible debt, and convertible preferred stock. Choice B is incorrect because authorized but unissued shares are simply part of the corporate charter and do not represent existing contracts that could result in common share issuance.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Evaluate dilutive character of options across interim periods
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Eastgate Corp. has 50,000 stock options with an exercise price of $45. In Q1, the average market price was $60, making the options dilutive. In Q2, the average market price fell to $40. Evaluate the proper diluted EPS treatment of these options across the two interim periods.', '["Include in diluted EPS for both quarters because the options were dilutive in at least one quarter during the fiscal year", "Include in diluted EPS for Q1 and exclude from Q2, because each interim period independently determines dilution based on its own average market price for that quarter", "Exclude from both quarters because the annual average market price of $50 should be recalculated and applied to each quarter retroactively", "Include in all quarters if the options are dilutive for the full annual period, regardless of individual quarter market prices"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22 and ASC 270, each interim period computes diluted EPS independently using the average market price for that specific period. In Q1 (AMP $60 > EP $45), the options are dilutive and included. In Q2 (AMP $40 < EP $45), the options are antidilutive and excluded. Choice A is incorrect because dilution is not carried forward from one interim period to another; each period stands on its own under ASC 260.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Convertible bonds with high coupon rate become antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Greenfield Corp. has net income of $600,000 and 300,000 common shares, producing basic EPS of $2.00. Greenfield also has $500,000 face value, 12% convertible bonds outstanding all year (tax rate 25%) converting into 15,000 common shares. Analyze whether these bonds are dilutive or antidilutive for the diluted EPS calculation.', '["The bonds are antidilutive because their per-share impact of $3.00 exceeds basic EPS of $2.00, and including them would increase diluted EPS to $2.05, above basic EPS", "The bonds are dilutive because any convertible security outstanding throughout the year must be included in diluted EPS under the if-converted method", "The bonds are dilutive because the after-tax interest savings of $45,000 always outweigh the additional 15,000 shares regardless of the EPS level", "The bonds are neither dilutive nor antidilutive because the per-share impact must be compared to diluted EPS from other securities, not to basic EPS"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, a security is antidilutive if its inclusion would increase EPS or decrease loss per share. After-tax interest = $500,000 x 12% x 0.75 = $45,000. Per-share impact = $45,000 / 15,000 = $3.00. Since $3.00 > basic EPS of $2.00, inclusion would yield ($600,000 + $45,000) / 315,000 = $2.05, which exceeds basic EPS. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 requires exclusion of antidilutive securities regardless of whether they were outstanding all year.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — When are options included in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, stock options are included in the diluted EPS calculation using the treasury stock method when which condition is met?', '["The options have been exercised during the current period", "The option holders have provided written notice of intent to exercise", "The exercise price is below the average market price during the period", "The exercise price is above the average market price during the period"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, stock options are included in diluted EPS when they are in-the-money, meaning the exercise price is below the average market price. The treasury stock method assumes hypothetical exercise regardless of actual exercise activity. Choice D is incorrect because when the exercise price exceeds the market price, the options are out-of-the-money and antidilutive, requiring exclusion from diluted EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Contingently issuable shares: conditions met mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Corp. acquired a subsidiary on January 1, Year 1 and agreed to issue 30,000 additional shares to the former owners if the subsidiary''s cumulative revenue exceeds $3 million. Cumulative revenue hit $3 million on September 1, Year 1. Whitfield has 200,000 common shares otherwise and net income of $500,000. Analyze how these shares should be treated in Year 1 basic versus diluted EPS.', '["Exclude from both basic and diluted EPS until the shares are formally issued to the former owners", "Include 10,000 weighted shares in basic EPS (30,000 multiplied by 4/12 from September 1) and 30,000 full-year shares in diluted EPS, because basic EPS weights from the date conditions are met while diluted EPS uses the year-end test", "Include 30,000 shares in both basic and diluted EPS for the full year because the revenue condition was achievable from January 1", "Include 30,000 shares in basic EPS from September 1 and exclude from diluted EPS because they are already in the basic computation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares enter basic EPS from the date all conditions are satisfied (September 1), weighted for the remaining period: 30,000 x 4/12 = 10,000. For diluted EPS under ASC 260-10-45-52, because the condition is met at the reporting date, the shares are included for the full year. Choice C is incorrect because basic EPS requires weighting from the date conditions are actually satisfied, not from the beginning of the year.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Evaluate inclusion of unvested options in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearpoint Financial granted 30,000 stock options on January 1, Year 1, with a three-year service vesting condition. The exercise price is $35 and the average market price is $70. Unamortized share-based compensation cost at December 31, Year 1 is $120,000. Evaluate whether these unvested options should be included in diluted EPS and the proper treatment of the unamortized compensation cost.', '["Exclude the options entirely until vested because unvested options are contingent consideration that should not affect diluted EPS", "Include all 30,000 option shares as incremental shares without applying the treasury stock method because unvested options have no cash exercise proceeds", "Include the options using the treasury stock method with assumed proceeds equal only to the cash exercise price of $1,050,000, producing 15,000 incremental shares", "Include the options using the treasury stock method with assumed proceeds of $1,170,000 (exercise price of $1,050,000 plus unamortized compensation of $120,000), producing approximately 13,286 incremental shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-26 and 45-29, unvested options are included in diluted EPS via the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds include exercise price plus unamortized compensation: $1,050,000 + $120,000 = $1,170,000. Shares repurchased = $1,170,000 / $70 = 16,714. Incremental = 30,000 - 16,714 = 13,286. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 includes unvested share-based payment awards in diluted EPS during the requisite service period, not only after vesting.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Evaluate unvested restricted shares with nonforfeitable dividends as participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeline Corp. grants 25,000 unvested restricted shares with four-year cliff vesting. The award agreement specifies that unvested shares receive the same per-share cash dividend as common stock, and these dividends are nonforfeitable even if the employee terminates before vesting. Ridgeline has 500,000 common shares and net income of $2,000,000. Evaluate whether these unvested restricted shares require the two-class method for basic EPS.', '["The restricted shares are participating securities requiring the two-class method because they receive nonforfeitable dividends, making them a separate class of security for earnings allocation purposes", "The restricted shares are not participating securities because they are unvested and therefore not legally outstanding common shares", "The restricted shares affect only diluted EPS through the treasury stock method and have no impact on basic EPS", "The restricted shares are participating securities only if total restricted share dividends exceed 5% of total common dividends"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A and ASC 718-10-25, unvested restricted shares that receive nonforfeitable dividends are participating securities because they share in undistributed earnings alongside common stock. The two-class method is required for basic EPS, allocating earnings between common shares and the participating restricted shares. Choice B is incorrect because participation in earnings through nonforfeitable dividends — not legal share outstanding status — triggers the two-class method under ASC 260.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Definition of the if-converted method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does the if-converted method assume when computing diluted EPS for convertible securities?', '["The convertible securities are redeemed for cash at the beginning of the period", "The convertible securities were converted into common stock at the beginning of the period or date of issuance if later, with corresponding adjustments to the numerator and denominator", "The convertible securities are exercised at the average market price during the period", "The convertible securities are settled at their fair value on the reporting date"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes conversion at the beginning of the period (or issuance date if later). The numerator is adjusted to remove effects of the convertible (e.g., add back after-tax interest for bonds or add back dividends for preferred), and the denominator includes the shares that would have been issued upon conversion. Choice C is incorrect because the if-converted method does not use market price — that is the treasury stock method used for options and warrants.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Shares issued for acquisition: weighted from acquisition date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinehurst Corp. acquired Larkspur Inc. on October 1, Year 1 by issuing 80,000 new common shares. Before the acquisition, Pinehurst had 400,000 shares outstanding all year. Analyze the proper treatment of the acquisition shares in Pinehurst''s annual basic EPS calculation.', '["Include 80,000 shares for the full year because the acquired business contributed earnings throughout the year through its standalone operations", "Include 80,000 shares retroactively adjusted to January 1 because business combination share issuances are treated like stock dividends under ASC 260", "Include 80,000 shares weighted from October 1 (approximately 20,000 shares) because shares issued in a business combination are included from the acquisition date, matching the period the acquiree''s results are consolidated", "Exclude the 80,000 shares entirely because acquisition-related share issuances are presented separately from ongoing EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued in a business combination are weighted from the acquisition date because the acquiree''s earnings are included in consolidated income only from that date. Weighted impact = 80,000 x 3/12 = 20,000 additional weighted shares. Choice A is incorrect because including shares for the full year would misalign the denominator timing with the acquiree''s earnings contribution, which begins only at the acquisition date.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L2 — Basic EPS with simple facts
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornbury Inc. reports net income of $450,000 for the year. The weighted-average number of common shares outstanding is 150,000 and there are no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$2.50", "$3.50", "$4.50", "$3.00"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, basic EPS equals income available to common shareholders divided by the weighted-average common shares. EPS = $450,000 / 150,000 = $3.00. There are no preferred dividends to deduct, so the full net income is available to common shareholders. Choice A ($2.50) is incorrect because it does not reflect the correct quotient of $450,000 divided by 150,000 shares.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Diluted EPS with in-the-money options
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hargrove Corp. has net income of $800,000, 200,000 weighted-average common shares, no preferred dividends, and 20,000 stock options with an exercise price of $40. The average market price during the year was $80. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.81", "$4.00", "$3.64", "$3.48"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method calculates incremental shares: exercise proceeds = 20,000 x $40 = $800,000; shares repurchased = $800,000 / $80 = 10,000; incremental = 20,000 - 10,000 = 10,000 shares. Diluted EPS = $800,000 / (200,000 + 10,000) = $800,000 / 210,000 = $3.81. Choice B ($4.00) is incorrect because it equals basic EPS and fails to include the 10,000 incremental dilutive shares from the in-the-money options.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Evaluate contingently convertible bonds for diluted EPS inclusion
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Holdings issued $2,000,000 of contingently convertible bonds. The bonds convert into 50,000 common shares only if Westbrook''s stock price exceeds $80 for 20 consecutive trading days. The stock price never reached $80 during Year 1, and the average market price was $65. The bonds carry a 3% coupon with a tax rate of 25%. Evaluate the proper diluted EPS treatment of these bonds.', '["Exclude from diluted EPS because the stock price trigger was never met during Year 1, so conversion is not currently possible", "Include in diluted EPS only for the portion of the year when the stock price exceeded $60", "Include in diluted EPS using the if-converted method because ASC 260 requires contingently convertible instruments to be included regardless of whether the market price contingency has been met, provided the effect is dilutive", "Include in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method rather than the if-converted method because the contingent conversion feature makes them economically similar to options"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35 through 45-43 (as amended by ASU 2020-06), all convertible instruments — including contingently convertible bonds — use the if-converted method for diluted EPS. The market price contingency does not gate inclusion; the bonds are included if the effect is dilutive. After-tax interest = $2,000,000 x 3% x 0.75 = $45,000; PSI = $45,000 / 50,000 = $0.90. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 does not condition diluted EPS inclusion on whether the contingent trigger has been met during the period.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Share repurchase authorization vs actual repurchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Maplebrook Corp.''s board authorized a buyback of up to 100,000 shares on November 1, Year 1. By December 31, Year 1, only 20,000 shares had been repurchased on December 1. Total shares before any repurchases were 500,000. Analyze the Year 1 EPS impact of the authorization and actual repurchase.', '["All 100,000 authorized shares reduce the weighted-average denominator because the board authorization commits the company to repurchase", "Only the 20,000 actually repurchased shares affect EPS, reducing the weighted-average by approximately 1,667 shares (20,000 weighted for one month), because EPS reflects only shares actually outstanding during the period", "The full 100,000 shares reduce diluted EPS through the reverse treasury stock method but do not affect basic EPS", "Neither the authorization nor the actual repurchase affects Year 1 EPS because both events occurred in the last two months of the year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, only actual changes in shares outstanding affect the weighted-average computation. The 20,000 shares repurchased on December 1 reduce the weighted-average by 20,000 x 1/12 = approximately 1,667 shares. The remaining 80,000 authorized but unexecuted shares have no effect. Choice A is incorrect because a board authorization to repurchase shares is not an actual reduction in outstanding shares and does not affect the EPS denominator under ASC 260.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Prior-period EPS restatement for error correction
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakdale Corp. originally reported basic EPS of $3.50 for Year 1 based on net income of $875,000 and 250,000 weighted-average shares. In Year 2, Oakdale discovered an error that overstated Year 1 net income by $175,000. Analyze the effect on Year 1 EPS when presented comparatively in the Year 2 financial statements.', '["Year 1 EPS is not restated because it was correct based on information available at the time of issuance", "Year 1 EPS is restated only in the footnotes, with the original $3.50 remaining on the face of the comparative income statement", "Year 1 EPS is restated by reducing the weighted-average shares from 250,000 to offset the income error", "Year 1 EPS is restated from $3.50 to $2.80 on the face of the comparative income statement because ASC 250 requires retrospective correction of errors, reducing the numerator by $175,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-1 and ASC 260-10-45-2, prior-period errors require retrospective restatement of all affected financial data, including EPS. Restated net income = $875,000 - $175,000 = $700,000. Restated EPS = $700,000 / 250,000 = $2.80. Choice A is incorrect because material errors are corrected retrospectively under ASC 250 regardless of whether the original amounts were based on available information; the correction must flow through to all affected per-share amounts.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Weighted-average shares with stock dividend and subsequent issuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashford Corp. had 150,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On March 1, Ashford declared a 10% stock dividend. On October 1, Ashford issued 30,000 new shares for cash. Net income was $690,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$4.60", "$4.00", "$3.54", "$3.83"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, the stock dividend is applied retroactively to the entire period: 150,000 x 1.10 = 165,000. The October 1 issuance is weighted for the remaining quarter. Weighted-average = (165,000 x 9/12) + (195,000 x 3/12) = 123,750 + 48,750 = 172,500. Basic EPS = $690,000 / 172,500 = $4.00. Choice A ($4.60) is incorrect because it uses the pre-dividend share count of 150,000, ignoring the retroactive stock dividend adjustment required by ASC 260.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
